WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.260
- [Narrator] This is
the Texas horned lizard,

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.750
a species which happens to be
the official state reptile,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.762
and you know what they
say, “Don't Mess With Texas.”

00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:13.345
(gentle music)

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.090
The Texas horned lizard is aptly named

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.840
because of its numerous horns.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.470
The most prominent horns
are the two central ones

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.273
located at both the center
and rear of the skull.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.320
Frequently preyed upon by other animals,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.330
its distinct coloration allows it

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.160
to camouflage itself amongst vegetation.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.540
But when hunted by creatures

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.760
such as snakes, coyotes, and hawks,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.440
they can either inflate to a larger size

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.960
or flatten in order to
blend in with the ground.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.970
However, their best and more
unique defense against prey

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.210
is their ability to shoot a
stream of blood from its eyelid,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.173
directly into the enemy's eyes.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.860
The Texas horned lizard's population

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.860
has dropped dramatically in recent years

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.430
due to the state's rapid urbanization,

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.440
leaving them with a shrinking habitat.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.143
This is the Texas horned lizard.

